const faqs = [
  { q: 'What are the four specifications I must specify for an OEM battery?', a: 'Four things: (1) the standard family and case group (e.g. JIS D26 or DIN H6), (2) capacity in Ah at the C20 rate, (3) CCA and its test basis (JIS at &minus;15&deg;C vs EN/SAE at &minus;18&deg;C), and (4) terminal type and polarity. Miss any one and the battery may not fit or perform as quoted.' },
  { q: 'Why does a JIS CCA number look higher than an EN CCA for the same battery?', a: 'Because JIS tests at &minus;15&deg;C while EN/SAE test at &minus;18&deg;C — a warmer test produces a higher number. A battery rated 600 CCA (JIS) and 540 CCA (EN) can be the same physical battery, so the values are not interchangeable.' },
  { q: 'How many cells make up a 12V car battery?', a: 'Six series-connected lead-acid cells, each nominally 2 V, giving a 12 V system. A 24 V heavy-truck system uses twelve cells — either one 24 V battery or two 12 V batteries in series.' },
  { q: 'Is Ah or CCA more important?', a: 'They measure different things and both belong in a specification. CCA is peak starting current for cold cranking; Ah is total stored energy (at the 20-hour rate). A cold-climate diesel needs CCA; a high-accessory vehicle needs Ah.' },
  { q: 'Does the BCI group size apply outside North America?', a: 'The BCI group number is a North American convention, but its dimensions overlap other standards — BCI Group 48 equals DIN/EN H6/LN3 at 278 &times; 175 &times; 190 mm. Knowing the mapping lets one factory serve every market from one mould set.' },
];
